meta {
  name: List Unanchored Policies
  type: http
  seq: 5
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/clients/unlinked?kind=unanchored&page=1&pageSize=25
  body: none
  auth: inherit
}

params:query {
  kind: unanchored
  page: 1
  pageSize: 25
}

headers {
  ~X-Branch-Id: {{branchId}}
}

tests {
  test("returns 200 OK", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });

  test("returns a paginated envelope discriminated by kind", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(body.kind).to.equal("unanchored");
    expect(body).to.have.property("page");
    expect(body).to.have.property("pageSize");
    expect(body).to.have.property("total");
    expect(body).to.have.property("totalPages");
    expect(body.items).to.be.an("array");
  });

  test("the envelope is internally consistent", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(body.items.length).to.be.at.most(body.pageSize);
    expect(body.totalPages).to.equal(
      Math.max(1, Math.ceil(body.total / body.pageSize)),
    );
  });

  test("agrees with the count on the chip", function () {
    const counted = bru.getVar("unanchoredPolicyCount");
    if (counted !== undefined) {
      expect(res.getBody().total).to.equal(counted);
    }
  });

  test("rows carry the household and no dates", function () {
    const items = res.getBody().items;
    if (items.length > 0) {
      const row = items[0];
      expect(row).to.have.property("id");
      expect(row).to.have.property("policyNumber");
      expect(row).to.have.property("policyType");
      expect(row).to.have.property("carrier");
      expect(row).to.have.property("premium");
      // The household is what makes the row actionable — unlike `kind=policies`,
      // where its absence is the defect being reported.
      expect(row).to.have.property("householdId");
      expect(row).to.have.property("householdRef");
      expect(row).to.have.property("householdName");
      // Every date on one of these rows would be null by definition, so none is
      // carried. A date column here would be three empty columns.
      expect(row).to.not.have.property("effectiveDate");
      expect(row).to.not.have.property("expirationDate");
      expect(row).to.not.have.property("renewalDate");
      expect(row).to.not.have.property("isTestRecord");
      expect(row).to.not.have.property("agencyId");
    }
  });
}

docs {
  # GET /clients/unlinked?kind=unanchored

  **Active policies with no renewal anchor** — the fourth chip on the Unlinked
  tab (PAC-126).

  - **Module:** `clients`
  - **Permission:** `clients:read` (see `Unlinked Counts`)
  - **Data scope:** `own` collapses to **branch**

  ## What the predicate is, and why

  `active: true` **and** `renewalDate`, `effectiveDate` and `expirationDate` all
  absent.

  `policies.renewalDate` is the date every renewal call counts backwards from.
  The scan derives it through the chain `renewalDate ?? effectiveDate ??
  expirationDate`; when all three are empty there is nothing to derive a term
  from, `nextRenewalDate` returns null, and the policy is skipped on every pass
  **forever**. The 2026-09-08 backfill counted exactly this population and
  deliberately refused to invent a date — "a policy with neither anchor is left
  alone and reported rather than given a guessed date that would call real
  clients". This list is that report, in the app.

  The predicate mirrors the **runtime** chain rather than the backfill's
  (`effectiveDate ?? deal.soldDate`), so a row disappears from the list exactly
  when outreach starts seeing it. Restricted to `active` because an inactive
  policy gets no outreach, which also makes the count honest as a queue: the
  number of live policies the renewal desk currently cannot see.

  Rides the `{agencyId, active, renewalDate}` index the renewal scan added.

  ## Response
  `{ kind, page, pageSize, total, totalPages, items: UnanchoredPolicyRow[] }`

  A row is `id`, `policyNumber`, `policyType`, `carrier`, `policyStatus`,
  `premium`, `items`, `householdId`, `householdRef`, `householdName`,
  `createdAt`. Sorted newest first.

  **No date fields**, deliberately — all three are null by construction. What
  makes a row actionable is *whose* policy it is, so the household is looked up
  instead (after paging: one indexed `_id` hit per rendered row). It is nullable,
  because a policy can be unattributed *and* undated, and so appear in this list
  and `kind=policies` at once.

  Rows exclude `isTestRecord` policies.

  ## What the client does with a row
  Opens `/clients/:householdId` and edits the policy on its card
  (`PATCH /households/:id/policies/:policyId`). Entering the **effective date**
  re-derives the renewal anchor server-side, which is what removes the row.
  A row with no household falls back to `/policies/:id`.

  ## Errors
  | Status | When |
  |---|---|
  | 400 | `kind` outside the vocabulary, `page`/`pageSize` out of bounds |
  | 401 | Missing/expired bearer token |
  | 403 | `clients` disabled, or the caller lacks `clients:read` |
}
